{0 jscontact}

JSContact for OCaml. A typed representation of contact data as defined by
{{:https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9553.html}RFC 9553}, with
{{:https://erratique.ch/software/jsont}jsont} codecs for every object type the
specification defines.

JSContact is the JSON contact data model that succeeds vCard. It is the payload
of the [ContactCard] object of
{{:https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9610.html}JMAP for Contacts (RFC 9610)},
and this library implements that payload.

{1 The entry point}

{!Jscontact} is the single module of the library. Everything below is reached
through it, as {!Jscontact.Card}, {!Jscontact.Name} and so on.

{2 The Card}

{!Jscontact.Card} is the topmost object of JSContact data and the one an
application starts from. Its properties are the sections of
{{:https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9553.html#section-2} RFC 9553 Section 2}.

- {!Jscontact.Name} for the name, its components and the nicknames, Section
  2.2.1 and Section 2.2.2.
- {!Jscontact.Org} for the organizations, job titles and how to address the
  contact, Section 2.2.3 to Section 2.2.5.
- {!Jscontact.Contact} for the email addresses, online services, phone numbers
  and preferred languages, Section 2.3.
- {!Jscontact.Calendar} for the calendaring resources and scheduling addresses,
  Section 2.4.
- {!Jscontact.Address} for the postal addresses and geographic locations,
  Section 2.5.
- {!Jscontact.Resource} for the cryptographic keys, directories, links and
  media, Section 2.6.
- {!Jscontact.Info} for the relations, anniversaries, notes and personal
  information, Section 2.1.8 and Section 2.8.

{2 The data types}

- {!Jscontact.Id}, the identifier that keys the repeated properties of a Card,
  Section 1.4.1.
- {!Jscontact.Date}, the UTCDateTime and the partial dates of an anniversary,
  Section 1.4.5 and Section 2.8.1.
- {!Jscontact.Patch}, the PatchObject that localizes a Card, Section 1.4.3.
- {!Jscontact.Unknown}, the members an object codec does not define, Section
  1.7.4.
- {!Jscontact.Vendor}, the grammar of a vendor-specific name or value, Section
  1.8.
- {!Jscontact.Registry}, the registered and reserved property names, Section
  1.7.

{2 The common properties and syntaxes}

- {!Jscontact.Context}, the [contexts] property, Section 1.5.1.
- {!Jscontact.Pref}, the [pref] property, Section 1.5.3.
- {!Jscontact.Phonetic}, the phonetic system and script properties, Section
  1.5.4.
- {!Jscontact.Language}, the language tags of
  {{:https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5646.html} RFC 5646}.
- {!Jscontact.Uri}, the URIs of
  {{:https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3986.html} RFC 3986}.

{2 Building codecs and validators}

- {!Jscontact.Valid}, the combinators each [validate] function is written with.
- {!Jscontact.Enum}, the functor every enumerated value type is built with.
- {!Jscontact.Json}, the codecs for the [@type] member, the UnsignedInt type and
  the JSON objects {{:https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9553.html} RFC 9553}
  uses as maps and sets.

{1 Unknown properties survive}

Section 1.7.4 requires an implementation to preserve a property it does not
understand rather than drop it, and Section 1.8.1 requires the same of a
vendor-specific property such as [example.com:foo]. Every object keeps them in
an [unknown] field of type {!Jscontact.Unknown.t} and writes them back out, so a
Card passes through this library without losing data an extension put there.

{1 Enumerated values are open}

Section 1.7.5 lets any enumerated property carry a vendor-specific value, so
each enumeration is a polymorphic variant with a [`Vendor of string] case. A
value from a later JSContact version decodes rather than failing, and [validate]
is where it is held to the vendor grammar.

{1 Shape versus meaning}

A codec enforces the {e shape} of an object. That is the type of each property
and the presence of the mandatory ones. It does not enforce the rules
{{:https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9553.html} RFC 9553} states in prose, such
as "at least one of the name and units properties MUST be set", "the value MUST
be in the range of 1 to 100", or "MUST NOT be set if the isOrdered property
value is false". Those live in the [validate] function of each type.

A Card a server sends therefore decodes into a usable value even if it breaks a
rule. An application that creates or edits a Card calls [validate] to hold
itself to Section 1.7.

{1 Localizing a Card}

Section 2.7.1 stores the property values of other languages in the
[localizations] property, as one {!Jscontact.Patch.t} per language tag.
{!Jscontact.Card.localize} applies one. It copies the Card without its
localizations, applies every patch of the PatchObject, and sets the language of
the copy.

{1 Reading and writing}

The codecs are {{:https://erratique.ch/software/jsont}jsont} values. With the
[jsont.bytesrw] library a Card is read from and written to a string as follows.

{[
  let card = Jsont_bytesrw.decode_string Jscontact.Card.jsont s in
  let s = Jsont_bytesrw.encode_string Jscontact.Card.jsont card in
  ...
]}

{1 vCard}

The [jscontact.vcard] library converts between a Card and a {!Vcard.t} by the
rules of {{:https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9555.html}RFC 9555}. See
{!Jscontact_vcard}.
